Patch 28: give the pre-trellis gate a margin finer than a power of two.

Supersedes patch 0027 -- apply INSTEAD of it. Standalone, against anchor
44be072 ("Add early termination in warp delta refinement", #5309).

WHAT 0027 MEASURED. At Speed 3, with the margin at shift 3 (1/8 of the
candidate's own cost):

    A1  +3.79% / +0.09% BD   ratio 42.1    bar 25, passes by 1.7x
    A2  +3.03% / +0.15% BD   ratio 20.2    bar 25, misses by 4.8

and at shift 4 (1/16), both classes got faster and worse:

    A1  +4.71% / +0.17% BD   ratio 27.7
    A2  +5.40% / +0.37% BD   ratio 14.6

A1 passed in every arm run, including both Speed 4 arms (28.5 and 44.7). A2
binds everywhere. Speed 4 should be dropped for this family: its best A2 result
was 9.2, and its absolute speedups were ~1-2%.

WHICH DIRECTION TO MOVE. The marginal ratio of the shift 3 -> shift 4 step is

    A1:  (4.71-3.79) / (0.17-0.09) = 11.5
    A2:  (5.40-3.03) / (0.37-0.15) = 10.8

Both are far below the ratio each class is already operating at (42.1 and 20.2).
A change only improves a ratio if its marginal ratio is above that ratio, so
tightening the margin moves the whole family the wrong way -- which is exactly
what the shift 4 arms showed. **The correct direction is to loosen it.**

HOW FAR. Solving for the point where A2 reaches the bar, at A2's own observed
marginal ratio of 10.8:

    give up 0.55 of A2's 3.03% speedup  ->  (+2.48%, +0.099%)  =  ratio 25.0
    the same proportional give-up on A1 ->  (+3.11%, +0.031%)  =  ratio ~100

That is an 18% reduction in speedup. **The problem is that the next power-of-two
step is nothing like 18%.** Measured locally (192x128, 4 frames, QP 185,
cpu-used=3, three paired reps against the same baseline binary), speedup
retained relative to the shift-3 setting:

    MARGIN64  8  (= shift 3)   +17.68%   100%
    MARGIN64 10                +14.60%    83%     <- the 18% give-up, near exactly
    MARGIN64 12                +13.39%    76%
    MARGIN64 16  (= shift 2)    +8.44%    48%     <- far too coarse

Going to shift 2 gives up 52% of the speedup to buy a quality improvement that
only needed 18% of it.

WHAT THE PATCH DOES. Nothing but reparameterize the margin from a shift to a
fraction in 64ths, so settings between the two powers of two CTC has already
bracketed become reachable:

    -DAVM_TX_PRE_TRELLIS_GATE_MARGIN64=4    drop if  6.7% worse  (= old shift 4)
    -DAVM_TX_PRE_TRELLIS_GATE_MARGIN64=8    drop if 14.3% worse  (= old shift 3)
    -DAVM_TX_PRE_TRELLIS_GATE_MARGIN64=10   drop if 18.5% worse  (NEW)
    -DAVM_TX_PRE_TRELLIS_GATE_MARGIN64=12   drop if 23.1% worse  (NEW, default)
    -DAVM_TX_PRE_TRELLIS_GATE_MARGIN64=16   drop if 33.3% worse  (= old shift 2)
    -DAVM_TX_PRE_TRELLIS_GATE_MARGIN64=0    gate disabled

THE REPARAMETERIZATION IS EXACT, AND THAT IS CHECKED, NOT ASSERTED. Built at
MARGIN64=8 and run against the 0027 shift-3 binary on the same clip, the two
produce byte-identical bitstreams:

    0027 shift 3     md5 bd66192aa502bbec   476 bytes
    0028 MARGIN64=8  md5 bd66192aa502bbec   476 bytes

so K=10 and K=12 sit on the same curve as the CTC points already measured, and
the arms below are directly comparable to the 0027 numbers above.

WHAT TO RUN. Speed 3 only, two arms: MARGIN64=12 (default) and MARGIN64=10.
Nothing at Speed 4.

WHAT I EXPECT, AND WHY IT IS A RANGE NOT A NUMBER. If the 83% / 76% speedup
retention transfers from the local clip and BD-rate falls at the observed
marginal ratio, A2 lands near 24.6 at K=10 and near 28.0 at K=12. Those
figures come from a two-point linear fit extrapolated backwards, and BD-rate
cannot keep falling linearly as it approaches zero -- the model certainly
over-predicts at the low end, which is why K=12 rather than K=10 is the
default. **The honest prediction is that A2 lands somewhere in 24-30 at K=12,
and A1 comfortably above the bar in both arms.**

WHAT WOULD FALSIFY IT. If A2 at K=12 comes back below 25 while its speedup is
down by the expected quarter, then BD-rate is not falling with the prune count
-- the damage is coming from a small number of prunes that survive at every
margin, and no setting of this knob will fix it. In that case the gate needs
the bias removed rather than padded, which is patch 0029.

diff --git a/av2/encoder/tx_search.c b/av2/encoder/tx_search.c
index be3b148..6665826 100644
--- a/av2/encoder/tx_search.c
+++ b/av2/encoder/tx_search.c
@@ -2626,6 +2626,90 @@ static void search_tx_type(const AV2_COMP *cpi, MACROBLOCK *x, int plane,
                                    tx_sf->prune_intra_ist_stx_by_zero_eob)) {
           continue;
         }
+        // Pre-trellis RD gate.
+        //
+        // The trellis (RDOQ, or the 8-state TCQ path) is the single most
+        // expensive step in this loop -- a callgrind profile of the encoder
+        // puts trellis quantisation at ~47% of instructions retired -- and it
+        // runs once per candidate transform type, per secondary-transform
+        // index, per transform block. Only one candidate survives.
+        //
+        // The FP-quantised coefficients for this candidate are already sitting
+        // in qcoeff after av2_quant above. Cost them, take the transform-domain
+        // distortion, and drop the candidate before the trellis runs if even
+        // that estimate cannot come near the incumbent.
+        //
+        // The estimate is PESSIMISTIC about the candidate: the trellis exists
+        // to lower rate, so the true post-trellis RD is below this one, while
+        // best_rd is already a post-trellis figure. Comparing the two directly
+        // would prune candidates that would have won. The margin pays for that
+        // bias -- the candidate is dropped only when it is worse than the
+        // incumbent by more than MARGIN64/64 of its own cost.
+        //
+        // The margin was a power-of-two shift in the first version of this
+        // gate. CTC showed that to be too coarse a control. At shift 3 (a
+        // 1/8 margin, MARGIN64 == 8):
+        //
+        //     A1  +3.79% / +0.09% BD  ratio 42.1   (bar 25, passes by 1.7x)
+        //     A2  +3.03% / +0.15% BD  ratio 20.2   (bar 25, misses by 4.8)
+        //
+        // and at shift 4 (a 1/16 margin, MARGIN64 == 4) both classes got
+        // faster and worse: A1 27.7, A2 14.6. The marginal ratio of that step
+        // is 11.5 on A1 and 10.8 on A2 -- far below the ratio each class is
+        // already operating at -- so the whole family moves the wrong way when
+        // the margin is tightened, and the correct direction is to LOOSEN it.
+        //
+        // How much? At A2's observed marginal ratio of 10.8, giving up 0.55
+        // percentage points of its 3.03% speedup lands it at
+        // (+2.48%, +0.099%) = ratio 25.0 exactly, and takes A1 to roughly
+        // (+3.11%, +0.031%) = ratio 101. That is an 18% reduction in speedup.
+        // The next power-of-two step, shift 2, doubles the margin and (on a
+        // local paired measurement) makes the gate fire so rarely that it no
+        // longer repays its own cost -- far more than an 18% give-up.
+        //
+        // So the margin is now in 64ths, and the interesting settings are
+        // between the two powers of two that CTC has already bracketed:
+        //
+        //     MARGIN64  4   drop if  6.7% worse   (= old shift 4, measured)
+        //     MARGIN64  8   drop if 14.3% worse   (= old shift 3, measured)
+        //     MARGIN64 10   drop if 18.5% worse   (NEW)
+        //     MARGIN64 12   drop if 23.1% worse   (NEW, default)
+        //     MARGIN64 16   drop if 33.3% worse   (= old shift 2)
+        //     MARGIN64  0   gate disabled entirely
+        //
+        // ("drop if X worse" is the threshold on pre_rd/best_rd, i.e.
+        //  64/(64-MARGIN64) - 1.)
+        //
+        // Note this is the same test the existing line below already performs
+        // ("if rd cost based on coeff rate alone is already more than best_rd,
+        // terminate early") -- but that one runs AFTER the trellis has been
+        // paid for, so it saves only the distortion call. This one runs before.
+        //
+        // Excluded: 64-point transforms, where three quadrants of coefficients
+        // are forced to zero and dist_block_tx_domain systematically
+        // mis-estimates; FSC blocks, which take a different optimize path; and
+        // eob == 0, which the trellis would not be called on anyway.
+#ifndef AVM_TX_PRE_TRELLIS_GATE_MARGIN64
+#define AVM_TX_PRE_TRELLIS_GATE_MARGIN64 12
+#endif
+#if AVM_TX_PRE_TRELLIS_GATE_MARGIN64 > 0
+        if (quant_param.use_optimize_b && !fsc_mode_in && *eob != 0 &&
+            best_rd != INT64_MAX && txw != 64 && txh != 64) {
+          const int pre_rate = cost_coeffs(cm, x, plane, block, tx_size,
+                                           packed_tx_type, CCTX_NONE, txb_ctx,
+                                           cm->features.reduced_tx_set_used);
+          int64_t pre_dist, pre_sse;
+          dist_block_tx_domain(x, plane, block, tx_size, &pre_dist, &pre_sse);
+          const int64_t pre_rd = RDCOST(x->rdmult, pre_rate, pre_dist);
+          // pre_rd is bounded well below INT64_MAX/64 here (it is an RD cost
+          // for a single transform block), so the multiply cannot overflow.
+          const int64_t pre_rd_discounted =
+              pre_rd - ((pre_rd * AVM_TX_PRE_TRELLIS_GATE_MARGIN64) >> 6);
+          if (pre_rd_discounted > best_rd) {
+            continue;
+          }
+        }
+#endif  // AVM_TX_PRE_TRELLIS_GATE_MARGIN64 > 0
         if (fsc_mode_in && quant_param.use_optimize_b) {
           av2_optimize_fsc(cpi, x, plane, block, tx_size, packed_tx_type,
                            txb_ctx, &rate_cost);
